__registerContent("quickrecall/polity/exam-fraud-bill", {"notes":{"qr-exam-fraud-bill":{"era":"Quick Recall — Polity & Governance","significance":["Exam-integrity legislation is a high-relevance GS-2 governance topic, tied to NEET-UG and other paper-leak controversies","The 2026 Amendment Bill and its parent 2024 Act, the enhanced penalties, and the fast-track adjudication design are exam-worthy facts","It illustrates the gap between stringent law-on-paper and weak enforcement (zero convictions), a recurring governance theme"],"features":[{"key":"What & Why","main":"The Public Examinations (Prevention of Unfair Means) Amendment Bill, 2026 is a revised version of the Public Examinations (Prevention of Unfair Means) Act, 2024.\nAim: ensure time-bound investigations and speedy trials, create stronger deterrence against organised crime groups/institutions, and safeguard transparent, merit-based public examinations.","note":"Nodal ministry: Ministry of Education; the Union Education Minister piloted the Bill."},{"key":"Trigger / Context","main":"The NEET-UG 2024 exam saw question papers leaked in Bihar (Patna) and Hazaribagh (Jharkhand), affecting about 24 lakh students and triggering nationwide protests.\nCrucially, the 2024 Act did NOT secure a single conviction over its roughly two years of operation.","note":"This enforcement failure prompted the 2026 revision."},{"key":"Legislative Passage","main":"Passed by Parliament in 2026 (Lok Sabha, then the Rajya Sabha the next day) and sent to the President for assent.\nIt was introduced during the monsoon session amid the NEET controversy.","note":"It amends the 2024 Act rather than being a fresh standalone law."},{"key":"Investigation & Trial Design","main":"Investigations to be completed within 60 days by the CBI / central investigating agencies / a Special Task Force.\nTrials in designated special FAST-TRACK courts; the chargesheet must be filed within 3 months.\nAppeals go to the High Court, heard by a two-judge Division Bench, within 6 months.","note":"Aim: time-bound, speedy adjudication to create real deterrence."},{"key":"Enhanced Penalties","main":"General offences (Section 10(1)): imprisonment raised from 3–5 years to 5–10 years; fine for service providers raised to up to ₹50 lakh (from up to ₹10 lakh); debarment extended from 4 years to 8 years.\nOrganised crime networks (Section 11): minimum imprisonment raised from 5 to 7 years, with a fine of up to ₹1 crore.","note":"The Bill sharply escalates punishment for organised paper-leak syndicates."},{"key":"Concerns / Limitations","main":"PRS analysis: the Bill does NOT prescribe a rigid outer limit for the conclusion of criminal proceedings (only for investigation/chargesheet).\nA Supreme Court ruling (2002) held that rigid trial timelines are neither advisable nor feasible.\nFast-track courts face system-wide pendency (e.g., POCSO special courts: ~257 days average trial in Andhra Pradesh vs ~1,717 days in Delhi).\nLaw Commission-noted bottlenecks: absence of the accused, non-attendance of police, weak judicial case management, and inadequate court/staff strength.","note":"Stringent penalties may not help if structural enforcement gaps persist."}],"traps":[{"label":"2024 Act vs 2026 Amendment","lines":["Parent law: Public Examinations (Prevention of Unfair Means) Act, 2024.","2026 Bill AMENDS the 2024 Act; it enhances penalties and adds fast-track adjudication.","Key failure of the 2024 Act: zero convictions in ~2 years."]},{"label":"Timelines","lines":["Investigation: within 60 days; chargesheet: within 3 months; appeal (HC Division Bench): within 6 months.","No rigid outer limit for CONCLUSION of the trial (per PRS)."]},{"label":"Penalty figures","lines":["General offences: 5–10 years; service-provider fine up to ₹50 lakh; debarment up to 8 years.","Organised crime (Section 11): minimum 7 years; fine up to ₹1 crore."]}],"mainsAngle":"The 2026 Amendment reflects a familiar governance instinct — respond to a high-profile failure (NEET-UG leaks) by escalating penalties and promising speedy justice. But the 2024 Act's zero-conviction record shows that deterrence depends less on harsher punishment on paper and more on investigative capacity, prosecutorial competence and judicial case-management. Without addressing these structural bottlenecks, stringent timelines risk remaining aspirational.","mainsTheme":"Law-on-paper vs enforcement capacity in curbing examination malpractice","recall":["Parent Act: Public Examinations (Prevention of Unfair Means) Act, 2024","2026 Bill amends the 2024 Act; nodal ministry: Education","Trigger: NEET-UG 2024 paper leaks (Bihar, Hazaribagh); ~24 lakh students","2024 Act: zero convictions in ~2 years","Investigation ≤60 days (CBI/agency/STF); chargesheet ≤3 months; HC appeal ≤6 months","General offences 5–10 yrs, fine up to ₹50 lakh; organised crime min 7 yrs, fine up to ₹1 crore","PRS: no rigid outer limit for trial conclusion; fast-track courts face pendency"]}},"quizzes":{"qr-exam-fraud-bill":{"title":"Public Examinations (Prevention of Unfair Means) Amendment Bill, 2026","questions":[{"q":"The Public Examinations (Prevention of Unfair Means) Amendment Bill, 2026 is a revised version of which law?","options":["The Right to Education Act, 2009","The Public Examinations (Prevention of Unfair Means) Act, 2024","The National Testing Agency Act, 2017","The University Grants Commission Act, 1956"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 2026 Bill amends and revises the Public Examinations (Prevention of Unfair Means) Act, 2024."},{"q":"Which examination controversy is cited as the key trigger for the 2026 Bill?","options":["CUET 2023 glitches","NEET-UG 2024 paper leaks","UPSC 2022 delays","JEE 2021 postponement"],"correct":1,"explain":"The NEET-UG 2024 paper leaks (in Bihar and Hazaribagh, Jharkhand), affecting about 24 lakh students, triggered the revision."},{"q":"A major criticism of the 2024 Act, motivating the 2026 revision, was that it:","options":["Was struck down by the Supreme Court","Did not secure a single conviction in about two years of operation","Applied only to private examinations","Had no penalty provisions"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 2024 Act failed to secure even a single conviction over roughly two years, exposing an enforcement gap."},{"q":"Under the 2026 Bill, investigations are to be completed within:","options":["30 days","60 days","90 days","180 days"],"correct":1,"explain":"Investigations are to be completed within 60 days by the CBI, central investigating agencies, or a Special Task Force."},{"q":["Consider the following timelines proposed in the Bill:","I. Chargesheet must be filed within 3 months.","II. Appeals to the High Court must be heard within 6 months by a two-judge Division Bench.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — a 3-month chargesheet deadline and a 6-month High Court (Division Bench) appeal timeline are provided."},{"q":"For general offences under Section 10(1), the Bill raises imprisonment to:","options":["1–3 years","3–5 years","5–10 years","10–15 years"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Bill raises imprisonment for general offences from 3–5 years to 5–10 years."},{"q":"For organised crime networks under Section 11, the minimum imprisonment is raised to:","options":["3 years","5 years","7 years","10 years"],"correct":2,"explain":"The minimum imprisonment for organised crime networks is raised from 5 to 7 years, with a fine of up to ₹1 crore."},{"q":"The maximum fine for service providers under the enhanced anti-cheating provisions is:","options":["₹10 lakh","₹25 lakh","₹50 lakh","₹1 crore"],"correct":2,"explain":"The fine for service providers is raised to up to ₹50 lakh (from up to ₹10 lakh under the 2024 Act)."},{"q":"Trials under the Bill are to be conducted in:","options":["Ordinary district courts","Designated special fast-track courts","The Supreme Court directly","Consumer courts"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Bill provides for trials in designated special fast-track courts to ensure speedy adjudication."},{"q":"Which agencies are empowered to investigate offences under the Bill?","options":["Only the local police","The CBI, central investigating agencies, or a Special Task Force","Only the National Testing Agency","The Election Commission"],"correct":1,"explain":"Investigations may be conducted by the CBI, central investigating agencies, or a Special Task Force, within 60 days."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the 2026 Bill:","I. It prescribes a rigid outer time limit for the conclusion of criminal trials.","II. It sets timelines for investigation and chargesheet filing.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":1,"explain":"Only II is correct. Per a PRS analysis, the Bill does NOT prescribe a rigid outer limit for the conclusion of the trial, though it sets investigation/chargesheet timelines."},{"q":"The Bill's debarment period for offenders is extended to:","options":["2 years","4 years","6 years","8 years"],"correct":3,"explain":"The debarment period is extended from 4 years to 8 years."},{"q":"Which Ministry is the nodal ministry for the Bill?","options":["Ministry of Home Affairs","Ministry of Education","Ministry of Law and Justice","Ministry of Personnel"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Ministry of Education is the nodal ministry; the Union Education Minister piloted the Bill."},{"q":"Assertion (A): Harsher penalties alone may not curb exam malpractice.\nReason (R): The 2024 Act, despite stringent provisions, secured no convictions due to structural enforcement gaps.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R explains A — the 2024 Act's zero-conviction record shows that deterrence depends on enforcement capacity, not just harsher law on paper."},{"q":"According to the Law Commission, common ground-level bottlenecks delaying such trials include all EXCEPT:","options":["Absence of the accused","Non-attendance of police","Deficiencies in judicial case management and court/staff strength","Excessive number of acquittals ordered by the Supreme Court"],"correct":3,"explain":"The bottlenecks cited are absence of the accused, non-attendance of police, and weak judicial case management/court strength — not Supreme Court acquittals."},{"q":"The Bill was passed by Parliament and then sent to whom for assent?","options":["The Chief Justice of India","The President of India","The Attorney General","The Election Commission"],"correct":1,"explain":"After passage by both Houses, the Bill was sent to the President of India for assent to become law."},{"q":"The primary purpose of the 2026 Bill is to:","options":["Abolish national entrance examinations","Ensure time-bound investigations, speedy trials, and stronger deterrence against organised exam-fraud","Transfer all exams to state governments","Reduce the number of public examinations"],"correct":1,"explain":"Its primary purpose is to ensure time-bound investigations and speedy trials and to create stronger deterrence against organised crime groups involved in exam malpractice."},{"q":"The wide variation in fast-track court trial durations (e.g. ~257 days in Andhra Pradesh vs ~1,717 days in Delhi) was cited in the context of:","options":["NEET result declaration","POCSO special courts, to illustrate systemic pendency challenges","Election petitions","Income-tax appeals"],"correct":1,"explain":"The example of POCSO special courts' widely varying trial times was used to illustrate the systemic pendency challenge that fast-track courts face."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The 2026 Bill enhances penalties for both individual offenders and organised crime networks.","II. It provides for appeals to be heard by a two-judge Division Bench of the High Court.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — the Bill raises penalties for individuals (Section 10) and organised networks (Section 11) and routes appeals to a two-judge High Court Division Bench."},{"q":"Which of the following best captures the core governance lesson of the exam-fraud law debate?","options":["Stringent penalties automatically eliminate malpractice","Effective deterrence requires investigative capacity, prosecutorial competence and judicial case-management, not just harsher penalties","Public examinations should be discontinued","Only state governments can prevent exam fraud"],"correct":1,"explain":"The debate shows that deterrence depends on enforcement capacity and speedy, competent adjudication — harsher penalties alone are insufficient (as the 2024 Act's record demonstrates)."}]}}});
